/// The operational infrastructure API for **in-world controllers**: a device the embedder grants
/// this interface (`DeviceOptions::infra`), such as the `replicas` controller app, creates,
/// starts, stops, removes, sends commands to, lists, watches and reads the logs of sibling
/// devices while the world runs.
///
/// **Every mutation is a derived operator event.** A call never changes the world inside the
/// call: it validates, records what was asked (the resource shows it as its `target`), journals
/// an `infra-<verb>` event on the caller, and queues the change in the simulation's one event
/// queue at the caller's own position (the processing context of the call, like a frame
/// transmitted with zero latency; a graceful stop at `now + grace`). The change applies when the
/// world reaches that event, before anything it causes, so it is deterministic, recomputed on
/// replay (never recorded as an operator input), and a debugger can stop the world between the
/// request and its effect. Reads (`get`, `list`, `watch`, `logs`, `events.read`) are answered
/// from the world as it is at the call. `changes.wait` and `changes.wait-any` are the functions
/// that do not return at once: they wait, on virtual time, for a change in the caller's view
/// (`wait-any` also for an event of a device it created).
///
/// **No new authority.** The host serves this interface only to devices whose
/// `DeviceOptions::infra` is set, and refuses to instantiate any other component that imports
/// it. It reaches no host resource. A controller may change only the devices it created (`owner`); it
/// reads the network's own devices and the ones it created, not other controllers' (its view of
/// the table). A device spec it applies can grant nothing (no `infra`).
///
/// Specs are CBOR (deterministic encoding): a device as the embedder describes it, without its
/// name, plus its cables:
///
/// ```text
/// { "component": "node:http-server",          ; a catalog name, or a digest reference
///                                               ; "sha256:<64 hex>" of a component the
///                                               ; caller supplied (`components.supply`)
///   "config":    { "mac": "02:..", "app.http-server.listen": "0.0.0.0:80", ... },
///                                               ; text values; nested maps flatten with "."
///   ? "stdio":   "journal",                     ; stdout/stderr lines into the journal
///                                               ; (what `logs` reads)
///   ? "args":    [ text ],                      ; arguments after argv[0] (the name), <= 256
///   ? "env":     { "NAME": "value", ... },      ; environment variables, <= 256, sorted
///                                               ; by name; both served by wasi:cli/environment;
///                                               ; each text <= 4 KiB, no NUL, no `=` in a name
///   ? "fuel_per_instant": uint,
///   ? "memory":  uint,                          ; the most linear memory it may use, bytes, all
///                                               ; its instances together: growing past it traps
///                                               ; (it crashes alone, `crashed`)
///   ? "wire":    [ cable ] }
///
/// cable := { "port": uint, "to": "sw:3" / "sw", ? "latency": uint (ns), ? "mtu": uint,
///            ? "impair": impairment / [ impairment, impairment ] }
///                                               ; "to" without a port: the first free port
///                                               ; of that device when the change applies;
///                                               ; one impairment for both directions, or two by
///                                               ; sending end (from this device first)
/// impairment := { ? "rate": uint (bits/s), ? "queue": uint (frames), ? "loss_ppm": uint,
///                 ? "jitter": uint (ns) }       ; what is left out is unimpaired
/// ```
///
/// A cable's far end is a device the caller created or one of the network's own, never another
/// controller's: the one rule for cables of a creation and of `devices.wire`.
///
/// No other system's types cross this interface: a controller translates whatever it speaks
/// into these specs.
///
/// Versions: 0.1.1 added `devices.control`; 0.1.2 added `changes.wait`, `devices.control-answer`,
/// `devices.wire`, `devices.unwire`, `components`, `events`, the spec keys `args`, `env` and
/// `memory`, digest references as a spec's `component` and the cable key `impair`. A component
/// built against
/// 0.1.0 or 0.1.1 still links, since an addition keeps the `0.1` line compatible.
package wasmnet:infra@0.1.2;

interface types {
    /// Virtual time in nanoseconds since the simulation started.
    type instant = u64;

    /// A resource version: the ordinal of the last change that touched a device, among the
    /// changes in the caller's view of the world's device table (1 for the first change; 0:
    /// nothing has changed yet). The list's `rv` is the latest change. Monotonic within a run,
    /// the same on every replay, and the same when the world is split over sites.
    type rv = u64;

    /// Labels: `(key, value)` pairs, sorted by key, keys unique.
    type labels = list<tuple<string, string>>;

    /// What a device is doing.
    enum phase {
        /// Asked for (by `apply`) and not created yet.
        pending,
        running,
        /// Stopped (by `stop` or an operator): no store, its links down, its name kept.
        stopped,
        /// It trapped: stopped by its own fault (its links down, `message` the trap). Only devices
        /// a controller created crash alone; a trap anywhere else fails the whole run. `start`
        /// runs it again.
        crashed,
        /// Removed: unwired, its name free. Only seen in a `deleted` change.
        removed,
        /// The last request could not be applied (`message` says why): apply again or remove.
        failed,
    }

    /// One device, as the world has it.
    record device {
        name: string,
        /// Stable for the device's life (an `apply` that recreated it keeps it): the digest of
        /// the event that created it, so reruns and forks with the same history agree.
        uid: string,
        /// 1 at creation, +1 per spec change (an `apply` that recreated it).
        generation: u64,
        /// The change that last touched it.
        rv: rv,
        labels: labels,
        /// The controller that created it; `none`: the network's description or an operator.
        owner: option<string>,
        /// Its spec (CBOR, see the package docs): as applied, or as asked while `pending`.
        spec: list<u8>,
        phase: phase,
        /// Where the requests still queued for it lead (`running`, `stopped`, `removed`);
        /// `none` when nothing is queued.
        target: option<phase>,
        /// When it entered its phase.
        since: instant,
        /// Its apps that returned, by name: `ok` or `error` (a composed node's `app-exit`). Only
        /// devices a controller created list them; the network's own devices list none.
        apps: list<tuple<string, string>>,
        /// Why the last request about it failed, if it did.
        message: option<string>,
    }

    /// How a change touched a device.
    enum change-kind {
        added,
        modified,
        deleted,
    }

    /// One watch event: the device as of that change (`device.rv`).
    record change {
        kind: change-kind,
        device: device,
    }

    /// One line a device wrote (the device needs `DeviceOptions::journal_stdio`).
    record log-line {
        at: instant,
        /// `stdout` or `stderr`.
        %stream: string,
        line: string,
    }

    /// The answer to a command (`devices.control-answer`, 0.1.2).
    record command-answer {
        /// The device it went to, and the command.
        name: string,
        command: string,
        /// When it came: the instant the command ran in the device (or was refused).
        at: instant,
        /// The device's `ok` text or its refusal (or why the command never reached it).
        answer: result<string, string>,
    }

    /// What `apply` did.
    record applied {
        device: device,
        /// `false`: the device already had this spec and labels (nothing was queued).
        changed: bool,
    }

    variant error {
        /// The name is taken by a device with another owner, or a request id was reused with
        /// other arguments.
        conflict(string),
        /// No such device, or no such component in the caller's store.
        not-found(string),
        /// A malformed spec, name, selector, stream or command, or a command for a device that
        /// is not running.
        invalid(string),
        /// Not this caller's to change (another owner, the caller itself), or a spec that
        /// would grant authority (`infra`), or a name outside the caller's name prefix; also every
        /// call from a device that was not granted this interface.
        denied(string),
    }
}

/// The devices of the caller's world. Every function is **sync and never yields**; the host
/// stamps everything with virtual time. Arguments and results are owned by the caller (lists
/// are copied across the boundary).
///
/// Mutations (`apply`, `start`, `stop`, `remove`, `control`) take an optional `request-id`: the
/// host keeps the last 256 answers per caller, so a retry with the same id and arguments is
/// answered from the record and never applied twice; the same id with other arguments is a
/// `conflict`. Every mutation but `control` is idempotent by name besides: asking for what is
/// already true (or already queued) queues nothing.
interface devices {
    use types.{instant, rv, labels, phase, device, change, log-line, applied, command-answer, error};

    /// Creates the device `name` from `spec` (CBOR) with `labels`, or updates it: a device with
    /// another spec is recreated (removed and added again with its new cables, in one change:
    /// same uid, generation + 1); one with other labels only gets the labels at once. A new
    /// device starts running when the change applies (there is no separate start: a later
    /// `start` is a no-op). Returns the device as asked for (`pending`, or with its `target`).
    /// Fails with `conflict` if `name` belongs to another owner (or to the network's
    /// description), `invalid` for a bad spec, `denied` for a spec that grants authority or
    /// names a digest reference from a caller not granted `components`, `not-found` for a
    /// digest reference the caller's store does not keep.
    apply: func(request-id: option<string>, name: string, labels: labels, spec: list<u8>) -> result<applied, error>;

    /// Starts a stopped or crashed device again (a fresh instance of its component and config; the links
    /// its stop took down come back up). A running or pending device: a no-op.
    start: func(request-id: option<string>, name: string) -> result<device, error>;

    /// Stops a running device after `grace-ns` of virtual time (0: at once): it keeps running
    /// for the grace period, then its store is dropped and its links go down (the far ends see
    /// the carrier drop). Its name, spec and cables stay. A stopped device: a no-op.
    stop: func(request-id: option<string>, name: string, grace-ns: u64) -> result<device, error>;

    /// Removes a device (stopped if running, unwired, its name free), after any change still
    /// queued for it (so `stop` with a grace period, then `remove`, removes it after the grace
    /// period). `false`: there was no such device (a successful no-op).
    remove: func(request-id: option<string>, name: string) -> result<bool, error>;

    /// Sends `command` with `argument` to the `wasmnet:control/commands` export of a device this
    /// caller created (a router's `route-add` with `10.0.3.0/24 via 10.0.2.2`), which changes it
    /// in place: its frames in flight, its state, its links and their captures stay, as they
    /// would not if it were recreated.
    ///
    /// - Like every mutation it only records and queues: the command runs in the device when the
    ///   derived event applies, at `now` and after any request still queued for the device (so an
    ///   `apply` followed by a `control` reaches the new device), and the device is polled at that
    ///   instant after it. Returns the device as asked for (`target`: `running`).
    /// - The device's answer is not returned by this call: it comes later, when the command
    ///   runs. With a request id, `control-answer` reads it from then on. Either way the answer
    ///   is a `modified` change of the device (which wakes `changes.wait`); a refusal (the
    ///   device's `err`, or a device that stopped meanwhile or takes no commands) is also
    ///   journalled on the caller as `infra-failed {verb: control, name, message}` at the
    ///   instant it is known, and shown as the device's `message`; what a command did is in the
    ///   device's own journal.
    /// - Idempotent by request id only: without one, every call sends the command again, so a
    ///   device's commands should be idempotent themselves (the router's are).
    /// - Fails with `not-found` for no such device; `denied` for a device another owner or the
    ///   network's description has, or the caller itself; `invalid` for a command that is not
    ///   1 to 63 lowercase letters, digits and `-` starting with a letter, an argument over
    ///   64 KiB, or a device that is not running or about to run.
    control: func(request-id: option<string>, name: string, command: string, argument: string) -> result<device, error>;

    /// The answer to the command this caller sent with `request-id` (0.1.2): `none` while it has
    /// not run yet, then the device's `ok` text or its refusal (or why the command never reached
    /// the device), at most 64 KiB (longer answers are cut). Answers are the caller's own; the
    /// host keeps the last 256 commands each caller sent with a request id. It is answered from
    /// the instant the command runs (the derived event's own instant), the same on every replay
    /// and in a world split over sites. Fails with `not-found` for a request id the caller sent
    /// no command with, or one more than 256 commands ago.
    control-answer: func(request-id: string) -> result<option<command-answer>, error>;

    /// Plugs `cable` (CBOR, a spec's cable, see the package docs) into port `cable.port` of
    /// `name`, a device this caller created (0.1.2). Its far end follows the one rule of
    /// cables: a device the caller created, or one of the network's own.
    ///
    /// - Like every mutation it only records and queues: the cable is plugged in when the
    ///   derived event applies, at `now` and after any request still queued for `name` (so an
    ///   `apply` followed by a `wire` reaches the new device), with its latency, MTU and
    ///   impairments set before any frame crosses it, and journalled as `link-added` on `name`.
    ///   A stopped end keeps the link down until it starts. Returns the device as asked for.
    /// - When it applies it fails (`infra-failed {verb: wire}`, and the device's `message`) for
    ///   a port that is taken or not declared, or a far device that is gone or has no free port.
    ///   In a world split over sites, a far end on another site fails the whole run there, at
    ///   that event (the same refusal as a creation's cable).
    /// - Idempotent by request id only (a second cable from the same port fails when it
    ///   applies). Fails with `not-found`, `denied` or `invalid` as `control` does, `denied` for
    ///   a far end another controller created, `invalid` for a malformed cable.
    wire: func(request-id: option<string>, name: string, cable: list<u8>) -> result<device, error>;

    /// Unplugs the cable at `port` of `name`, a device this caller created (0.1.2): the link
    /// goes down (frames in flight on it are lost) and both ports are free again; the link stays
    /// in the records with its capture, journalled as `link-removed` on `name`. Queued like
    /// `wire`; when it applies it fails if no cable is at that port, or if the cable leads to a
    /// device another controller created.
    unwire: func(request-id: option<string>, name: string, port: u32) -> result<device, error>;

    /// One device of the caller's view (including one still `pending`).
    get: func(name: string) -> result<device, error>;

    /// Every device of the caller's view whose labels include all of `selector`'s pairs (an
    /// empty selector: every one), sorted by name, with the latest `rv` (pass it to `watch` to see what changes
    /// next).
    %list: func(selector: labels) -> tuple<rv, list<device>>;

    /// The changes after `since` to devices matching `selector`, in world order, at most `max`
    /// of them, and the `rv` to resume from (the last change returned, or the latest if fewer
    /// than `max` matched). A pollable feed: a controller calls it on its period; nothing
    /// blocks. Pending requests are not changes (they show in `list` and `get`).
    watch: func(selector: labels, since: rv, max: u32) -> tuple<rv, list<change>>;

    /// The last `tail` lines (0: all) the device wrote to `stream` (`stdout` or `stderr`) at or
    /// after `since`, oldest first, of a device the caller created (`denied` for the network's own
    /// devices). Needs the device's `DeviceOptions::journal_stdio`, which a spec asks for with
    /// `"stdio": "journal"` (otherwise none).
    logs: func(name: string, %stream: string, since: instant, tail: u32) -> result<list<log-line>, error>;
}

/// A change feed that wakes its caller (0.1.2), for a controller that should react to a crash
/// or an exit at once instead of polling `watch` on a timer, and (`wait-any`) to an event of a
/// device it created. A controller without async tasks (a stepped device) arms the same wait with
/// `events.arm` instead, or polls `watch` from its `poll`.
interface changes {
    use types.{instant, rv, labels, error};
    use events.{follow, table-since, ready};

    /// Waits until the caller's view of the device table (see `devices`) has a change after
    /// `since` to a device whose labels include all of `selector`'s pairs, or until virtual
    /// time reaches `deadline` (`none`: no deadline), and returns the latest `rv` of the view;
    /// read the changes with `devices.watch(selector, since, ...)`.
    ///
    /// - Returns at once if such a change exists already (`since` older than the latest `rv`),
    ///   or if `deadline` is not after now.
    /// - Otherwise the caller's task waits; nothing else of the caller is blocked. A change
    ///   wakes it in the processing context the change is made in (a request of the caller
    ///   applying, a device's app exit, a crash), so the caller runs in that event's next round:
    ///   at the same position on every replay, and the same in a world split over sites.
    /// - Cancelling the call (dropping it) stops waiting and cancels its deadline.
    /// - Fails with `denied` from a device that was not granted this interface.
    wait: async func(selector: labels, since: rv, deadline: option<instant>) -> result<rv, error>;

    /// Waits until something in `table` (none: the table does not end the wait) or `follows`
    /// (at most 64 devices this caller created, see `events`) is ready, or until virtual time
    /// reaches `deadline` (none: no deadline), and says what is ready (nothing, at the
    /// deadline). A controller with one loop waits here for both its table and its devices'
    /// events (0.1.2).
    ///
    /// - Returns at once if something is ready already, or if `deadline` is not after now.
    /// - Otherwise the caller's task waits; nothing else of the caller is blocked. What ends the
    ///   wait (a device journalling an event, a change being made, a followed device being
    ///   removed) wakes it in that processing context, so the caller runs in that event's next
    ///   round: at the same position on every replay, and the same in a world split over sites.
    /// - Cancelling the call (dropping it) stops waiting and cancels its deadline.
    /// - Fails at once, without waiting, with `not-found`, `denied` or `invalid` as
    ///   `events.read` does for any follow, `invalid` for more than 64, `denied` from a device
    ///   that was not granted this interface.
    wait-any: async func(table: option<table-since>, follows: list<follow>, deadline: option<instant>) -> result<ready, error>;
}

/// The events of the devices a controller created (0.1.2): what each journalled through
/// `wasmnet:link/log.event` (a router's `tunnel-up {tunnel, peer}`, a switch's `mac-learned`, an
/// app's own events), so a controller can follow a device's state without sending it a command on
/// a period. Kinds and fields are the device's own text. The records the network makes about a
/// device (`device-added`, `app-exit`, ...) are not among them: the device table shows those.
///
/// **Whose events.** Only the devices the caller created: a device another controller created
/// is not in its view (`not-found`), and the network's own devices and the caller itself are
/// `denied`, as for `devices.logs`: in a world split over sites, a device of the network's own
/// may journal on another site, while the devices a controller created are on its site.
///
/// **What is kept.** The events of every device a controller created, from its creation (an
/// event it journals while it is instantiated included), in a ring per device: the last 256, at
/// most 256 KiB together (each event counting its kind, its field names and values and 32 bytes),
/// the newest always kept. Each field is at most 16 KiB, as in the journal. The ring goes with
/// the device when it is removed; a stop, a crash, a start and a recreation (`apply` with a new
/// spec) keep it.
///
/// **The cursor.** Every event has a number, `seq`: 1 for the first event of a device name, one
/// more for each next, never reused while the world runs (a device removed and created again
/// under its name goes on from the last number). A reader passes the last number it has seen as
/// `since` and gets what came after it: several events at one instant have numbers of their own,
/// so none is read twice or skipped. Events the ring dropped before a reader got them are
/// reported as `missed` (a gap, never skipped silently); a reader that missed events should read
/// the device's state another way (a router's `tunnels` command) and go on from `next`.
///
/// **Filters.** A list of kind patterns: a kind (`tunnel-up`), or a prefix and `*` (`tunnel-*`);
/// at most 16, each 1 to 128 bytes without spaces; an empty list is every kind.
///
/// **Waiting.** `changes.wait-any` (an async controller) and `arm` (a stepped controller, which
/// cannot await) end on one condition: a change of the device table the caller waits for, an
/// event a follow waits for, a gap after a follow's cursor, or a followed device leaving the
/// table. One call covers both the table and events, because a controller has one loop; the
/// async one lives in `changes`, so that this interface is all sync and a stepped device can
/// import it without async support. A wake comes in the
/// processing context of what caused it (the device journalling the event, the change being
/// made), so the controller runs at the same position on every replay and in a world split over
/// sites.
///
/// Served only to devices granted `wasmnet:infra`; every call of any other device is `denied`.
interface events {
    use types.{instant, rv, labels, error};

    /// One event a device journalled.
    record event {
        /// Its number among the events of the device's name (the cursor).
        seq: u64,
        /// When it was journalled.
        at: instant,
        kind: string,
        /// In the order the device gave them.
        fields: list<tuple<string, string>>,
    }

    /// What `read` found.
    record page {
        /// The events after `since` matching the filter, oldest first.
        events: list<event>,
        /// The cursor to pass as `since` next: every event up to it was looked at (those the
        /// filter left out included).
        next: u64,
        /// How many events after `since` the ring dropped before they were read, of any kind
        /// (the ring keeps every kind). Not 0: a gap.
        missed: u64,
    }

    /// One device a wait follows: events of `kinds` (empty: every kind) after `since`.
    record follow {
        name: string,
        kinds: list<string>,
        since: u64,
    }

    /// The table part of a wait: a change of the caller's view after `since` to a device whose
    /// labels include all of `selector`'s pairs, as `changes.wait` takes them.
    record table-since {
        selector: labels,
        since: rv,
    }

    /// What ended a wait.
    record ready {
        /// The latest resource version of the caller's view.
        rv: rv,
        /// The table has a change the wait was for.
        changed: bool,
        /// The followed devices with an event the wait was for, a gap, or gone from the table,
        /// in the order given. Read each with `read` (a gone one answers `not-found`).
        devices: list<string>,
    }

    /// The events device `name` journalled after `since` whose kind matches `kinds`, oldest
    /// first, at most `max` (0 counts as 1), with the cursor to read from next and the gap, if
    /// any. Sync, never yields; answered from the ring as it is at the call. A device still
    /// `pending` has none yet. Fails with `not-found` for no such device in the caller's view,
    /// `denied` for one the caller did not create, `invalid` for a malformed filter.
    read: func(name: string, kinds: list<string>, since: u64, max: u32) -> result<page, error>;

    /// `changes.wait-any` for a controller without async tasks (a stepped device), as a
    /// one-shot wake of its `wasmnet:link/device.poll`. Sync, never yields.
    ///
    /// - If something is ready already, returns it (`some`) and arms nothing: read and arm again.
    /// - Otherwise (`none`) it arms: the first event, change or departure that would end the wait
    ///   queues one more `poll` of the caller at that instant, in that processing context, after
    ///   what is already queued there. Its own poll deadlines are unaffected (the wake is a poll
    ///   of its own, beside them). It fires once; arm again after reading.
    /// - One armed wait per caller: a new `arm` replaces it, and one with no `table` and no
    ///   `follows` only disarms. The caller's stop, crash or restart disarms it too.
    /// - Fails like `changes.wait-any`, and then leaves nothing armed.
    arm: func(table: option<table-since>, follows: list<follow>) -> result<option<ready>, error>;
}

/// Components a controller supplies as bytes (0.1.2), for a controller that fetches a component
/// over the network (from a store node) and runs it. Served only to a controller the embedder
/// grants it (`DeviceOptions::infra_supply`, which needs `DeviceOptions::infra`); every call of
/// any other device is `denied` (`list`: empty).
///
/// A component is named by its **digest reference**, `sha256:` and the SHA-256 of its bytes in
/// 64 lowercase hex digits, which a spec's `component` may be (`devices.apply`): the device is
/// made from the caller's own bytes, never another controller's. The embedder decides what may
/// run: it judges every supplied component before anything keeps, compiles or instantiates it,
/// and a component that imports what the host does not serve fails its creation (`failed`, the
/// device's `message` names the import). A component is compiled once per digest, however many
/// devices are made from it.
///
/// **The store.** Each controller has its own: a component stays in it while the caller holds
/// it (from `supply` until `release`, or until the caller is removed), while a device of the
/// caller in the table names it (in any phase), or while a request still queued names it, and
/// goes (journalled as `component-dropped`) at the first point none is left. The embedder
/// bounds the largest component and the store's count and total bytes.
///
/// Every function is **sync and never yields**. Bytes are copied across the boundary; the host
/// never journals them, only their digest and size.
interface components {
    use types.{error};

    /// One component the caller's store keeps.
    record supplied {
        /// Its digest reference, `sha256:<64 hex>`.
        digest: string,
        /// Its size in bytes.
        size: u64,
        /// The caller holds it (`supply` until `release`).
        held: bool,
        /// How many of the caller's devices in the table and queued requests name it.
        users: u32,
    }

    /// Keeps `bytes`, a whole component, in the caller's store, and returns its digest
    /// reference; the caller holds it from now on.
    ///
    /// - `digest`: the reference the caller expects (it fetched it with the bytes); `none`: any.
    ///   The host computes the bytes' SHA-256 itself; a stated digest that differs is `invalid`
    ///   and nothing is kept.
    /// - Bytes the store keeps already: the caller holds them (again); they are not judged
    ///   again and cost no room.
    /// - Otherwise the embedder judges them, at this call: its refusal is `denied` with its
    ///   reason, and nothing is kept. Journalled as `infra-supply {digest, size}` on the caller
    ///   when it takes the hold.
    /// - Fails with `denied` from a caller without the grant, for a component larger than the
    ///   grant allows, or for a store already at its count or byte bound (release one, or remove
    ///   the devices that name one, first); `invalid` for a malformed `digest`.
    supply: func(digest: option<string>, bytes: list<u8>) -> result<string, error>;

    /// The caller no longer holds the component `digest` (journalled as `infra-release
    /// {digest}`): it goes once no device and no queued request of the caller names it either
    /// (at once if none does). `false`: the caller did not hold it (a successful no-op). Devices
    /// made from it keep running, and a stopped one keeps what it needs to start again. Fails
    /// with `denied` from a caller without the grant, `invalid` for a malformed `digest`.
    release: func(digest: string) -> result<bool, error>;

    /// The components the caller's store keeps, by digest (empty from a caller without the
    /// grant).
    %list: func() -> list<supplied>;
}

/// An in-world controller's capability: what the embedder grants a device it marks as a
/// controller.
world controller {
    import devices;
    import changes;
    import events;
    import components;
}
